═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  RESULTS — KC Python API as Verification Service
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  Storage (Flexo Layer 0 + Layer 1):
    Accepted ALL operations. Indifferent to semantics.

  Schema (Ontology Packages):
    kc-core + mtg-domain loaded into Flexo.
    Verification service gets schema from build_mtg_schema().

  Verification (KC Python API):
    Reconstructed each branch state via the KC API:
      add_vertex(), add_edge(), add_face() — domain-typed validation.
    Orphan detection via reconstruction gap.
    Named queries for domain analysis.

  This is the pattern a KerML/SysML v2 verification service
  would follow: domain-typed API, SHACL/SPARQL abstracted away.

  Compare branch-uv vs branch-vu for non-commutativity.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
